//- The fe design-system gates, computed once per page render. layout.pug
//- includes this ahead of _nav.pug (pug includes share one compiled scope, so
//- the nav reads these without recomputing them — and including this twice in
//- one chain would emit duplicate consts). email-api.pug builds its own html
//- element without going through layout.pug and includes this itself.

//- The bot check, once per render. isbot runs a large regex, and the
//- templates used to call it forty-odd times per page.

//- `/` only ever renders home.pug for a logged out visitor, since
//- auth.homeOrDomains redirects everyone else.
- const isFeLandingPage = !user && ctx.pathWithoutLocale === "/" && !boolean(ctx.query.pricing);
//- /download and /faq render their redesigned surfaces for signed in visitors
//- too: someone who already has an account is the most likely person to want
//- the app or the answers.
//- The secondary marketing pages joined the same list when they moved onto
//- the fe page header: they read the same signed in or out (the onboarding
//- form prefills the visitor's email either way).
- const isFeAccountAgnosticPage = ["/download", "/faq", "/free-email-webhooks", "/email-forwarding-regex-pattern-filter", "/disposable-addresses", "/reserved-email-addresses", "/ips", "/resources", "/blog/docs", "/guides"].includes(ctx.pathWithoutLocale);
//- The pricing template's hero renders under the same condition the template
//- itself uses: every logged out visitor gets it, ?pricing=true included —
//- the redesigned hero is compact enough that "jump to pricing" still lands
//- the table in view. Signed in visitors keep the previous design.
- const isFePricingPage = !user && (ctx.pathWithoutLocale === "/private-business-email" || Object.keys(useCases).includes(ctx.pathWithoutLocale));
//- body.fe-page scopes the dark fe modal treatment (_fe-modal.scss): every
//- logged out page, plus /download and /faq for signed in visitors. Signed in
//- dashboard modals instead follow the OS scheme via the --app-* tokens in
//- app-dark.scss, matching the chrome around them.
- const isFePage = !user || isFeAccountAgnosticPage;
//- Whether the page opens on a dark fe hero that runs under a transparent
//- navbar. _nav.pug keys the transparent bar on this flag. The FAQ is left
//- out: its topic rail starts at the top left, beside the hero, and in the
//- light scheme that column is light, which would put the white wordmark of a
//- transparent bar on a light ground. It keeps the opaque bar instead.
- const isFeFlushNav = isFeLandingPage || isFePricingPage || (isFeAccountAgnosticPage && !user && ctx.pathWithoutLocale !== "/faq");
//- Whether the page's dark fe hero starts at the top of the viewport, under
//- the fixed bar (the hero's own top padding already clears the bar); the
//- page templates read it to add .fe-landing--flush. True for every page with
//- a transparent bar, and for the account agnostic pages with an opaque one
//- (signed in, and the FAQ): a hero that did not pull up there sat below
//- body.app's padding, which left a strip of body background between the bar
//- and the hero that read as a thick second border, plus a doubled gap.
- const isFeHeroUnderBar = isFeFlushNav || isFeAccountAgnosticPage;
